  "query_preview": "..(Wednesday through Monday)\nIssued at 221 PM EDT MON SEP 7 2026\n\nThe period begins Wednesday morning with stout ridging over the \nsouthern half of the CONUS, while an active zonal jet stream remains \nacross the far northern CONUS. The ridge will progressively get \nshunted south and east through Thursday and into Friday as a \nprogressive shortwave pushes a cold front southeast into the \nsouthern Ohio Valley. However, it appears that the shortwave may \noutrun the surface front, leaving it to decay over or north of \neastern Kentucky into the weekend as mid-level ridging builds back \nover the region. \n\nThe NBM was kept for the extended period, with the exception of \nadding modest ridge-valley splits for Wednesday night into Thursday \nmorning ahead of the approaching system. Considerable uncertainty \narises in the greater synoptic pattern as the coming weekend \nprogresses into next week, particularly concerning the evolution of \na cut-off upper level low over the Pacific Northwest this weekend, \nwith downstream implications over the Midwest and Ohio Valley as \nensemble systems become increasingly out of phase with each other. \n\nWPC has introduced a Slight Risk ERO for Thursday into Thursday \nnight across much of WV and into northeastern and far eastern \nKentucky. This is as the approaching system Thursday into Friday \nwill be intruding into a warm and humid environment characterized by \nPWs peaking in the 90th-95th percentile (according to the LREF) \nacross much of the area northwest of the Cumberland Mountains. The \norientation of the front, the perpendicular upslope wind component \non the backside of the front, and the timing of arrival of heavy \nrain and/or thunderstorms at or near peak heating all suggest some \npotential for flash flooding potential along the windward side of \nthe Appalachians. \n\nPoPs are continued into the weekend as it appears the front decays \nover the region to end the week, with the potential for additional \nshortwaves and possibly weak surface lows to initiate at least \nspotty showers and thunderstorms across the area before mid-level \nheights likely rise Sunday into Monday of next week. \n\nThere is high confidence that temperatures remain above normal \nthrough the period, with the coolest day (and closest to normal \nreadings) coming Friday with the upper trough overhead. Highs will \ngenerally be in the mid to upper 80s with lows mostly in the 60s, \nthough a few lower 70s readings are possible Wednesday night in the \nwarm humid environment ahead of the approaching front.",
